          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+transforms+on+sheaves"} integral transforms on sheaves
            one thinks of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            as a generalized linear map. The
            %em span trace
            is the corresponding generalization of the notion of a trace of a linear map.
          %p
            This is just the general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
            which is definable in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact/autonomous
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal (2-)category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Span
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span
            is an example (as described below).
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            a useful aspect of the span trace is that it is manifestly dual to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-span+co-trace"} co-span co-trace
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#for_spans For Spans
          %p For
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
